1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,524
A support engineer
has to share a screenshot of a ticket,

2
00:00:03,524 --> 00:00:06,799
and it shows an email,
a phone number and a deploy token.

3
00:00:06,799 --> 00:00:11,713
SnapShackle covers them before the picture
leaves, and keeps the evidence.

4
00:00:11,713 --> 00:00:15,642
Everything on screen is invented,
and this is an expanding prototype.

5
00:00:15,892 --> 00:00:17,592
Here is how it works.

6
00:00:17,592 --> 00:00:22,461
It all runs on this one computer:
double-click the launcher and a PowerShell

7
00:00:22,461 --> 00:00:25,728
host on loopback
serves the studio to your browser.

8
00:00:26,429 --> 00:00:29,720
Captures and settings
stay in one local folder:

9
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:32,240
a PNG, and a small record beside it.

10
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:35,636
What goes in is pixels from this PC:

11
00:00:35,636 --> 00:00:40,429
a region, a window or a screen,
or an image you open or drop in.

12
00:00:41,129 --> 00:00:46,336
What comes out is a file you chose:
a flat PNG, the clipboard,

13
00:00:46,336 --> 00:00:49,779
or a copy into a share folder you set up.

14
00:00:50,479 --> 00:00:53,395
AI is optional, and off.

15
00:00:53,395 --> 00:00:55,302
The host calls Fairlead on

16
00:00:55,302 --> 00:01:00,364
loopback with SnapShackle's own key,
installed in Credential Manager.

17
00:01:00,364 --> 00:01:06,084
Lazarette holds Fairlead's provider key,
and is not linked to SnapShackle itself.

18
00:01:06,784 --> 00:01:11,184
Captures can go to Bowerbird, Capstan and
Scrimshaw as files with a manifest.

19
00:01:11,884 --> 00:01:14,784
Slack,
Teams and Jira upload are not built.

20
00:01:15,484 --> 00:01:20,993
And the guardrails: loopback and a session
token, nothing uploaded by

21
00:01:20,993 --> 00:01:26,741
SnapShackle, AI off until you turn it on,
and you tick every suggestion.

22
00:01:26,741 --> 00:01:31,116
To start from zero, run the start
script with its own data folder.

23
00:01:31,116 --> 00:01:33,457
It binds an available loopback port,

24
00:01:33,457 --> 00:01:36,838
here eighty-seven sixty-six,
and prints the address.

25
00:01:37,538 --> 00:01:40,613
Then make the evidence: a purpose-built

26
00:01:40,613 --> 00:01:45,737
window that exists only for this demo,
captured with PrintWindow,

27
00:01:45,737 --> 00:01:49,443
read by Windows OCR,
and checked for leftovers.

28
00:01:49,443 --> 00:01:50,684
Invented content only.

29
00:01:50,684 --> 00:01:53,979
Nothing else on this
desktop is ever in frame.

30
00:01:54,229 --> 00:01:59,940
Open the invented ticket window that
evidence capture made, then mark it up.

31
00:02:00,552 --> 00:02:03,810
An arrow at the token,
a box on the ticket line.

32
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:07,297
Undo is one step, and redo brings it back.

33
00:02:07,297 --> 00:02:09,215
Then numbered steps and a text note.

34
00:02:09,215 --> 00:02:11,940
Every mark stays a layer you can move.

35
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,792
Now the point of the tool.

36
00:02:14,492 --> 00:02:17,702
Find sensitive info
reads the image with Windows OCR.

37
00:02:18,402 --> 00:02:22,132
It finds the email,
the phone number and the API token,

38
00:02:22,132 --> 00:02:24,642
and lists each one to tick or untick.

39
00:02:25,342 --> 00:02:28,297
Redact checked covers all
three with solid boxes.

40
00:02:28,842 --> 00:02:31,175
Then redact every
instance of the project name.

41
00:02:31,875 --> 00:02:36,095
Export writes a flat PNG,
so the boxes are in the pixels.

42
00:02:36,795 --> 00:02:38,736
Save it to the library with
tags and a note.

43
00:02:39,436 --> 00:02:41,312
The saved record is what we check next.

44
00:02:42,062 --> 00:02:44,993
Search finds it by the words
inside the image,

45
00:02:44,993 --> 00:02:48,626
not just its name or tags:
ticket forty-eight twenty-one.

46
00:02:49,326 --> 00:02:54,601
On disk it is a PNG, and a small record
beside it, in this demo's own data folder.

47
00:02:55,301 --> 00:02:56,751
The record holds the tags,

48
00:02:56,751 --> 00:03:00,320
the note, and the text Windows
OCR reads from the saved picture.

49
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:04,075
The name,
the phone and the token are not in it.

50
00:03:04,775 --> 00:03:06,275
Reading the saved file

51
00:03:06,275 --> 00:03:10,912
again from the command line proves it:
no email, no phone, no token.

52
00:03:10,912 --> 00:03:13,835
That is the evidence trail.

53
00:03:14,085 --> 00:03:15,675
AI help goes through Fairlead.

54
00:03:15,675 --> 00:03:20,406
SnapShackle has its own client there,
so its calls can be told apart.

55
00:03:21,106 --> 00:03:23,618
Fairlead installed the key in Windows
Credential Manager.

56
00:03:24,318 --> 00:03:26,366
This lists the target only, never the key.

57
00:03:27,194 --> 00:03:28,934
Lazarette is not involved on this side.

58
00:03:29,634 --> 00:03:32,282
Open the saved
capture and turn AI help on.

59
00:03:32,282 --> 00:03:34,773
Privacy is local-only by default.

60
00:03:34,773 --> 00:03:36,729
For this demo I let the router

61
00:03:36,729 --> 00:03:41,880
pick any model, so the redacted picture
goes to a cloud model through Fairlead.

62
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:47,324
Describe image asks Fairlead, live,
and the alt text comes back in a moment.

63
00:03:48,024 --> 00:03:51,311
Suggest annotations returns a checklist,

64
00:03:51,311 --> 00:03:54,926
and the status bar
names the model: GPT-4.1.

65
00:03:54,956 --> 00:03:57,055
These are a model's guesses.

66
00:03:57,055 --> 00:03:58,274
It even offers to cover

67
00:03:58,274 --> 00:04:02,675
text that is already covered, so I untick
those and keep only the box and the step.

68
00:04:03,375 --> 00:04:07,833
Add checked puts them in as normal layers,
and one undo removes them all.

69
00:04:08,533 --> 00:04:12,411
And the evidence for the AI
call is Fairlead's own log:

70
00:04:12,411 --> 00:04:15,937
client SnapShackle,
the model, status two hundred.

71
00:04:15,937 --> 00:04:18,717
No image, and no prompt.

72
00:04:19,217 --> 00:04:20,402
Sharing is a file copy.

73
00:04:20,402 --> 00:04:23,713
Under Settings I name a folder,
here an outbox,

74
00:04:23,713 --> 00:04:26,390
and the studio never uploads anything.

75
00:04:27,090 --> 00:04:31,156
Select the capture,
choose that destination and share.

76
00:04:31,156 --> 00:04:32,946
A copy never overwrites an existing file.

77
00:04:33,646 --> 00:04:37,263
There it is in the outbox:
the same PNG the library holds.

78
00:04:37,263 --> 00:04:39,815
Nothing was sent anywhere else.

79
00:04:40,515 --> 00:04:45,377
For Bowerbird, Capstan or Scrimshaw,
the hand-off script copies chosen captures

80
00:04:45,377 --> 00:04:49,315
and writes a manifest with hashes,
tags and the recognised text.

81
00:04:50,015 --> 00:04:54,175
Their import is their side,
and I have not tested it here.

82
00:04:54,175 --> 00:04:56,019
The manifest holds no folder paths.

83
00:04:56,719 --> 00:04:59,903
End session
stops the host and releases the port.

84
00:04:59,903 --> 00:05:04,758
The window, the customer and the token
were all invented.

85
00:05:04,758 --> 00:05:10,549
Not built: Slack, Teams or Jira upload,
and a direct Lazarette link.

86
00:05:10,549 --> 00:05:14,297
OCR can miss text,
so look before you share.

87
00:05:14,297 --> 00:05:18,215
It still needs a run on a managed
workstation.

